  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Gaurav Manchanda vs Sh. Sanjay Khanna on 6 June, 2019 In The Court Of Sh. Dharmesh Sharma District & Sessions Judge (West) : Delhi Crl. Revision No. 105/2019 Cnr NoDLWT010024922019 Gaurav Manchanda S/o Sh. Sat Pal Manchanda R/o Wz75, Ramgarh Opp. Kirti Nagar, New Delhi 110015 ........... Revisionist Versus Sh. Sanjay Khanna S/o Sh. M. L. Khanna R/o H. No. 1/3190 A, First Floor, Ram Nagar Extention, Shahadra, Delhi ......... Respondent Date of filing : 29.03.2019 Date of arguments : 03.06.2019 Date of Order : 06.06.2019",
    "facts": "This Criminal Revision Petition has been filed by the revisionist/ complainant u/s 397 of the Code of Criminal Procedure assailing the impugned judgment dated 28.11.2018 passed by Ms. CR No.105/19 Gaurav Manchanda v. Sanjay Khanna Page 1of 4 Pryadarshani, Ld. MM01 (NI Act) West District, Delhi, whereby the accused has been acquitted of committing offence u/s 138 of the Negotiable Instrument Act 1881 (as amended up to date) and (hereinafter referred as the N. I. Act.). 2. Notice of the present petition was issued to the respondent accused by the Ld. Predecessor of this Court.",
    "arguments": "As regards the remedy of statutory appeal provided under Section 378(4) Cr.P.C., reliance can be placed on decision in Criminal Appeal No. 50 of 2013 titled Subhash Chand v. Delhi Administration, dated 8th January 2013, wherein in relation to a complaint u/s Section 20 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, CR No.105/19 Gaurav Manchanda v. Sanjay Khanna Page 2of 4 which offences under the PFA Act are both cognizable and non cognizable. It was observed: \"Thus, whether a case is a case instituted on a complaint depends on the legal provisions relating to the offence involved therein. Further, in Crl.A no. 972/2012 titled The Bhajanpura Cooperative Urban Thrift & Credit Society Ltd. v. Sushil Kumar decided on 3 September, 2014 by the High Court of Delhi, it was observed that \"the remedy available to the complainants under Section 138 N.I.Act against order of acquittal is only to seek special leave before filing an appeal under Section 378 (4) Cr.P.C. before the High Court\".",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "As regards the remedy of statutory appeal provided under Section 378(4) Cr.P.C., reliance can be placed on decision in Criminal Appeal No. 50 of 2013 titled Subhash Chand v. Delhi Administration, dated 8th January 2013, wherein in relation to a complaint u/s Section 20 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, CR No.105/19 Gaurav Manchanda v. Sanjay Khanna Page 2of 4 which offences under the PFA Act are both cognizable and non cognizable. It was observed: \"Thus, whether a case is a case instituted on a complaint depends on the legal provisions relating to the offence involved therein. Further, in Crl.A no. 972/2012 titled The Bhajanpura Cooperative Urban Thrift & Credit Society Ltd. v. Sushil Kumar decided on 3 September, 2014 by the High Court of Delhi, it was observed that \"the remedy available to the complainants under Section 138 N.I.Act against order of acquittal is only to seek special leave before filing an appeal under Section 378 (4) Cr.P.C. before the High Court\"."
  },
